title: decrypt_file creates a new plaintext output with umask permissions
updated: 2026-08-16T00:34:08Z
---

Split out of karr #40 rather than folded into it, because it is a permissions
policy question and #40 was a data-loss fix.

decrypt_file writes the DECRYPTED document. When its output file does not
exist yet it is created with 0666 & ~umask -- 0644 under the common umask 022,
i.e. world-readable plaintext secrets. That is what open ">" did before #40 and
what _replace_file deliberately reproduces now, so #40 changed nothing here.

Measured, sops 3.13.3, umask 022:

  sops -d --output dec-new.yaml enc.yaml   ->  dec-new.yaml is 0644

so the reference implementation is no better, and matching it was the
conservative choice for a fix whose whole point was not to surprise anyone.

The question this ticket is for: should decrypt_file (and edit, which already
uses 0600 for its temporary copy) create a NEW output at 0600 instead? The
argument for is that this distribution is a library called from code, the file
it just created is plaintext secrets, and 0600 is what its own edit path
already considers correct for exactly that content. The argument against is
that it is a deliberate divergence from sops that would break a caller whose
next step is another user or process reading the file -- loudly, at least.

If it is taken: it is a BEHAVIOUR CHANGE entry in Changes, a line in the
"How a file is written" POD section (which currently states the umask rule as
deliberate and says to set the umask or mode yourself), and it should probably
apply only to decrypt_file, not to encrypt_file, whose output is ciphertext.
POD + Changes now record the 'match sops, leave to caller' decision explicitly under 'How a file is written'. No code change.
